# Environment Configuration Implementation Guide

## Overview

Your application currently has **6 unused environment variables** in `.env.test` that are **NOT referenced** in any code. This document guides you through properly integrating environment variables.

---

## Current State vs Target State

### Current State (🔴 INSECURE)

```
app.js (CURRENT):
├─ app.listen(3000)           ← Hardcoded port
├─ No process.env usage       ← No config management
├─ No dotenv loading          ← Config not loaded
└─ No validation              ← No checks

.env.test:
├─ DATABASE_URL=...           ← Unused
├─ AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=...      ← Unused
├─ AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=...  ← Unused
├─ ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=...      ← Unused
├─ STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=...      ← Unused
└─ LOGFIRE_TOKEN=...          ← Unused
```

### Target State (🟢 SECURE)

```
app.js (IMPROVED):
├─ require('dotenv').config() ← Load .env
├─ process.env.PORT           ← Use from env
├─ process.env.NODE_ENV       ← Environment type
├─ process.env.DATABASE_URL   ← Database config
└─ Validation on startup      ← Check required vars

.env.example (in git):
├─ DATABASE_URL=              ← Template
├─ AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=         ← No values
├─ AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=     ← No values
├─ ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=         ← No values
├─ STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=         ← No values
└─ LOGFIRE_TOKEN=             ← No values

.env (NOT in git):
├─ DATABASE_URL=actual-secret  ← Real values
├─ AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=real-key  ← Real values
└─ etc...                      ← Local only
```

---

## Step-by-Step Implementation

### Step 1: Secure the Repository

#### 1.1 Remove .env.test from Git History

```bash
# See if .env.test is in history
git log --oneline -- .env.test

# Remove from history (if found)
git filter-branch --tree-filter 'rm -f .env.test' HEAD

# Or using git-filter-repo (recommended, faster)
git filter-repo --path .env.test --invert-paths

# Force push (CAUTION: affects all developers)
git push origin --force --all
```

#### 1.2 Update .gitignore

```bash
cat >> .gitignore << 'EOF'

# Environment variables
.env
.env.local
.env.test
.env.development
.env.production

# But DO track template
!.env.example
EOF

git add .gitignore
git commit -m "Add .env files to .gitignore"
```

#### 1.3 Verify Changes

```bash
# .env.test should not be tracked
git status

# .env files should be in .gitignore
cat .gitignore | grep env

# Verify .env.test is removed from history
git log --all --source --oneline -- .env.test
```

---

### Step 2: Create Environment Variable Templates

#### 2.1 Create .env.example (track in git)

```bash
cat > .env.example << 'EOF'
